1
00:00:04,599 --> 00:00:07,644
It begins as a trickle of meltwater, high

2
00:00:07,644 --> 00:00:11,133
on the Tibetan Plateau, more than five thousand

3
00:00:11,133 --> 00:00:12,693
metres above the sea.

4
00:00:13,193 --> 00:00:16,327
From there, it travels more than three thousand

5
00:00:16,327 --> 00:00:19,596
kilometres, through some of the highest mountains

6
00:00:19,596 --> 00:00:23,064
on Earth, and across the plains, to the Arabian Sea.

7
00:00:23,564 --> 00:00:26,482
Along the way, it built one of the world's

8
00:00:26,482 --> 00:00:29,470
first great civilisations, gave its name to

9
00:00:29,470 --> 00:00:32,388
a whole subcontinent, and today it feeds a

10
00:00:32,388 --> 00:00:35,237
nation of more than two hundred and forty

11
00:00:35,237 --> 00:00:36,279
million people.

12
00:00:36,779 --> 00:00:39,477
This is the story of the Indus.

13
00:00:50,177 --> 00:00:53,382
The Indus rises in western Tibet, near the

14
00:00:53,382 --> 00:00:56,586
sacred Mount Kailash and Lake Manasarovar.

15
00:00:56,586 --> 00:01:00,477
Tibetans call it the Senge Tsangpo, the Lion River.

16
00:01:00,977 --> 00:01:05,402
It flows north-west through Ladakh, in Indian-administered

17
00:01:05,402 --> 00:01:09,446
Kashmir, and then into Gilgit-Baltistan, in Pakistan.

18
00:01:09,946 --> 00:01:13,257
On the way, it gathers rivers fed by the great

19
00:01:13,257 --> 00:01:16,353
glaciers of the Karakoram and the Himalaya.

20
00:01:17,053 --> 00:01:20,249
Further west, the river carves its way through

21
00:01:20,249 --> 00:01:21,639
deep, narrow gorges.

22
00:01:22,139 --> 00:01:25,287
Here, the Indus passes beneath Nanga Parbat.

23
00:01:25,287 --> 00:01:28,434
At eight thousand one hundred and twenty-six

24
00:01:28,434 --> 00:01:32,297
metres, it is the ninth-highest mountain in the world.

25
00:01:32,797 --> 00:01:35,630
Then the river turns south, and begins its

26
00:01:35,630 --> 00:01:38,126
long journey down towards the plains.

27
00:01:38,826 --> 00:01:41,985
It leaves the mountains at Tarbela, and flows

28
00:01:41,985 --> 00:01:45,073
past Attock, where it is joined by the Kabul

29
00:01:45,073 --> 00:01:46,687
River from Afghanistan.

30
00:01:47,187 --> 00:01:50,370
In Punjab, five great rivers, the Jhelum,

31
00:01:50,370 --> 00:01:54,019
Chenab, Ravi, Beas and Sutlej, gather together,

32
00:01:54,019 --> 00:01:56,658
and join the Indus near Mithankot.

33
00:01:57,159 --> 00:02:00,447
That is where Punjab gets its name: the land

34
00:02:00,447 --> 00:02:01,568
of five waters.

35
00:02:02,068 --> 00:02:04,892
Then the Indus crosses Sindh, the province

36
00:02:04,892 --> 00:02:08,052
that takes its name from the river, and reaches

37
00:02:08,052 --> 00:02:10,271
the Arabian Sea, south of Thatta.

38
00:02:14,571 --> 00:02:17,406
Around four and a half thousand years ago,

39
00:02:17,406 --> 00:02:20,241
the banks of the Indus were home to one of

40
00:02:20,241 --> 00:02:22,805
the world's first great civilisations.

41
00:02:23,305 --> 00:02:26,662
Its two largest cities were Harappa, on the

42
00:02:26,662 --> 00:02:30,019
Ravi, and Mohenjo-daro, beside the Indus in

43
00:02:30,019 --> 00:02:32,829
Sindh. Both are in today's Pakistan.

44
00:02:33,329 --> 00:02:36,328
Mohenjo-daro may have been home to around

45
00:02:36,328 --> 00:02:39,619
forty thousand people, in a carefully planned

46
00:02:39,619 --> 00:02:42,618
city of brick houses, streets and drains.

47
00:02:43,318 --> 00:02:46,324
The civilisation stretched from Balochistan

48
00:02:46,324 --> 00:02:49,820
to near today's Delhi. It traded with Mesopotamia,

49
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:52,827
and it had its own script, which no one has

50
00:02:52,827 --> 00:02:54,365
yet been able to read.

51
00:02:54,865 --> 00:02:58,381
Harappa was identified in nineteen twenty-one,

52
00:02:58,381 --> 00:03:01,667
and Mohenjo-daro in nineteen twenty-two. In

53
00:03:01,667 --> 00:03:05,106
nineteen eighty, Mohenjo-daro became a UNESCO

54
00:03:05,106 --> 00:03:06,635
World Heritage Site.

55
00:03:07,335 --> 00:03:10,660
The river also gave its name to far more than

56
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:13,764
a civilisation. Its ancient Sanskrit name,

57
00:03:13,764 --> 00:03:16,867
Sindhu, gave us Sindh, and through Persian

58
00:03:16,867 --> 00:03:19,601
and Greek, the words Indus and India.

59
00:03:23,901 --> 00:03:26,806
In the nineteenth century, the British began

60
00:03:26,806 --> 00:03:29,644
building modern canals to carry the river's

61
00:03:29,644 --> 00:03:30,833
water to farmland.

62
00:03:31,333 --> 00:03:34,389
Today, the Indus and its tributaries feed

63
00:03:34,389 --> 00:03:37,892
what the World Bank calls the largest connected

64
00:03:37,892 --> 00:03:41,247
surface-water irrigation system in the world,

65
00:03:41,247 --> 00:03:44,154
with thousands of kilometres of canals.

66
00:03:44,654 --> 00:03:47,734
A chain of barrages along the river, from

67
00:03:47,734 --> 00:03:51,115
Chashma and Taunsa in Punjab to Guddu, Sukkur

68
00:03:51,115 --> 00:03:55,171
and Kotri in Sindh, turns the river into these canals.

69
00:03:55,671 --> 00:03:58,819
Much of Pakistan's wheat, rice, cotton and

70
00:03:58,819 --> 00:04:01,218
sugarcane depends on this water.

71
00:04:01,918 --> 00:04:05,222
In the north, the river is held back by Tarbela

72
00:04:05,222 --> 00:04:07,963
Dam, completed in nineteen seventy-six.

73
00:04:08,463 --> 00:04:11,578
It is one of the largest earth and rock filled

74
00:04:11,578 --> 00:04:14,353
dams in the world, and its lake stretches

75
00:04:14,353 --> 00:04:16,249
for about eighty kilometres.

76
00:04:16,749 --> 00:04:20,110
Tarbela stores water for the dry season, and

77
00:04:20,110 --> 00:04:22,402
generates hydroelectric power.

78
00:04:26,702 --> 00:04:29,857
When British India was partitioned in nineteen

79
00:04:29,857 --> 00:04:33,081
forty-seven, the new border cut straight across

80
00:04:33,081 --> 00:04:34,658
the Indus river system.

81
00:04:35,158 --> 00:04:38,309
The rivers rise in India or flow through it,

82
00:04:38,309 --> 00:04:41,675
but Pakistan's farms depend on them downstream.

83
00:04:42,175 --> 00:04:45,396
In nineteen sixty, after nine years of talks

84
00:04:45,396 --> 00:04:48,909
facilitated by the World Bank, the two countries

85
00:04:48,909 --> 00:04:51,178
signed the Indus Waters Treaty.

86
00:04:51,678 --> 00:04:54,724
Pakistan received the western rivers: the

87
00:04:54,724 --> 00:04:58,290
Indus, the Jhelum and the Chenab. India received

88
00:04:58,290 --> 00:05:02,302
the eastern rivers: the Ravi, the Beas and the Sutlej.

89
00:05:02,802 --> 00:05:06,016
The treaty survived wars and crises for more

90
00:05:06,016 --> 00:05:07,258
than sixty years.

91
00:05:07,958 --> 00:05:10,934
But in April twenty twenty-five, after an

92
00:05:10,934 --> 00:05:13,911
attack on tourists in Indian-administered

93
00:05:13,911 --> 00:05:17,105
Kashmir that killed twenty-six people, India

94
00:05:17,105 --> 00:05:20,590
announced it was placing the treaty in abeyance.

95
00:05:21,090 --> 00:05:24,447
India says this will last until Pakistan ends

96
00:05:24,447 --> 00:05:28,103
what it calls support for cross-border terrorism.

97
00:05:28,103 --> 00:05:31,535
Pakistan denies any involvement in the attack.

98
00:05:32,035 --> 00:05:35,353
On the thirty-first of August twenty twenty-six,

99
00:05:35,353 --> 00:05:38,326
the Court of Arbitration in The Hague ruled

100
00:05:38,326 --> 00:05:41,161
unanimously that the treaty remains fully

101
00:05:41,161 --> 00:05:44,895
in force, and that India cannot suspend it on its own.

102
00:05:45,395 --> 00:05:48,937
India did not take part in the case. It rejected

103
00:05:48,937 --> 00:05:52,774
the ruling, calling the court illegally constituted.

104
00:05:53,474 --> 00:05:56,756
Pakistan's prime minister, Shehbaz Sharif,

105
00:05:56,756 --> 00:06:00,272
has said that every single drop of Pakistan's

106
00:06:00,272 --> 00:06:01,834
water is a red line.

107
00:06:02,334 --> 00:06:05,559
For now, the treaty's future remains uncertain,

108
00:06:05,559 --> 00:06:08,509
and with it, the water on which millions of

109
00:06:08,509 --> 00:06:09,470
people depend.

110
00:06:13,770 --> 00:06:16,993
The Indus is also home to one of the rarest

111
00:06:16,993 --> 00:06:20,141
mammals on Earth: the Indus river dolphin,

112
00:06:20,141 --> 00:06:22,239
known locally as the bhulan.

113
00:06:22,739 --> 00:06:25,795
It is almost blind, and finds its way and

114
00:06:25,795 --> 00:06:28,777
its food in the muddy water using sound.

115
00:06:29,277 --> 00:06:32,509
It lives only in the Indus river system, and

116
00:06:32,509 --> 00:06:35,815
is listed as endangered. Its range has shrunk

117
00:06:35,815 --> 00:06:38,827
by around eighty percent, largely because

118
00:06:38,827 --> 00:06:41,913
barrages have cut the river into sections.

119
00:06:42,613 --> 00:06:45,863
A WWF-Pakistan survey in twenty seventeen

120
00:06:45,863 --> 00:06:49,509
counted one thousand eight hundred and sixteen

121
00:06:49,509 --> 00:06:52,997
dolphins, most of them between the Guddu and

122
00:06:52,997 --> 00:06:54,979
Sukkur barrages in Sindh.

123
00:06:55,479 --> 00:06:58,513
The good news is that their numbers have grown

124
00:06:58,513 --> 00:07:02,603
since the early two thousands, thanks to conservation efforts.

125
00:07:06,903 --> 00:07:09,897
At the end of its journey, the Indus spreads

126
00:07:09,897 --> 00:07:12,550
out into a vast delta, south of Thatta.

127
00:07:13,050 --> 00:07:16,280
But very little of the river now reaches the

128
00:07:16,280 --> 00:07:19,437
sea. Dams, barrages and canals take most of

129
00:07:19,437 --> 00:07:22,007
its water long before it gets here.

130
00:07:22,507 --> 00:07:25,777
Water researcher Hassan Abbas says the delta

131
00:07:25,777 --> 00:07:28,974
once received around one hundred and eighty

132
00:07:28,974 --> 00:07:32,616
to two hundred million acre-feet of water a year.

133
00:07:33,116 --> 00:07:35,761
Under a nineteen ninety-one agreement, it

134
00:07:35,761 --> 00:07:38,535
should now get at least ten million. But in

135
00:07:38,535 --> 00:07:41,696
twelve of the past twenty-five years, it did not.

136
00:07:42,396 --> 00:07:45,437
A twenty nineteen study by the US-Pakistan

137
00:07:45,437 --> 00:07:48,478
Center for Advanced Studies in Water found

138
00:07:48,478 --> 00:07:51,447
that the delta shrank from about thirteen

139
00:07:51,447 --> 00:07:54,489
thousand nine hundred square kilometres in

140
00:07:54,489 --> 00:07:57,675
eighteen thirty-three, to about one thousand

141
00:07:57,675 --> 00:08:00,209
and sixty-seven in twenty eighteen.

142
00:08:00,909 --> 00:08:03,911
That is a loss of ninety-two percent.

143
00:08:04,411 --> 00:08:07,404
As the river retreats, the sea moves in. In

144
00:08:07,404 --> 00:08:10,746
Kharo Chan, only three of its forty-two villages

145
00:08:10,746 --> 00:08:14,087
remain. The rest have been swallowed by the sea.

146
00:08:14,587 --> 00:08:17,548
Its population fell from twenty-six thousand

147
00:08:17,548 --> 00:08:20,306
in nineteen eighty-eight, to about eleven

148
00:08:20,306 --> 00:08:22,459
thousand in twenty twenty-three.

149
00:08:23,159 --> 00:08:26,115
From the glaciers of Tibet to the mangroves

150
00:08:26,115 --> 00:08:29,139
of Sindh, the Indus has shaped this land for

151
00:08:29,139 --> 00:08:30,445
thousands of years.

152
00:08:30,945 --> 00:08:34,060
It fed some of humanity's first cities. It

153
00:08:34,060 --> 00:08:37,100
feeds Pakistan today. And its future will

154
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:39,547
shape the lives of millions more.

155
00:08:40,047 --> 00:08:42,922
This was the story of the Indus. Thank you

156
00:08:42,922 --> 00:08:44,975
for watching Maps and Musings.
